<tr><td>Drive types supported</td><td>2.5" and 3.5" SATA</td><td>SATA (2.5/3.5) + NVMe M.2</td></tr>
<tr><td>USB interface</td><td>USB 3.0 (5 Gbps)</td><td>USB-C 3.2 Gen 2 (10 Gbps)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drive install time</td><td>30 seconds, plug the connectors</td><td>5 seconds, drop it in</td></tr>
<tr><td>Requires external power</td><td>For 3.5" drives yes; 2.5" no</td><td>For 3.5" drives yes; 2.5" no</td></tr>
<tr><td>Simultaneous drives</td><td>1</td><td>1 (some models: 2, offline clone)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Portability</td><td>Bag / pocket</td><td>Desk-only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>One-time data recovery</td><td>Frequent drive swapping</td></tr></tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When SABRENT SATA-to-USB Adapter Cable wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick SABRENT SATA-to-USB Adapter Cable if...</h2>
    <p>You need to grab files off one old laptop hard drive one time. The cable is $10, does the job, and lives in a drawer between uses. Buying a $40 dock for a single one-time job is wasteful.</p><p>You travel and might need to read a drive on the road. The cable fits in a bag. The dock does not.</p><p>You only work with SATA drives. If you don't own any NVMe drives, half the dock's feature set is unused. Get the cable and save the money.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When SABRENT Lay-Flat Drive Dock wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick SABRENT Lay-Flat Drive Dock if...</h2>
    <p>You pull drives regularly. IT work, data recovery as a hobby, homelab shuffling, drive testing before deploying — all of these become dramatically less annoying when you can drop a drive in without fumbling with cables.</p><p>You have any NVMe drives. NVMe requires a specific USB adapter (not SATA-to-USB); the dock reads both formats so you don't need two separate tools.</p><p>You want faster transfers. USB 3.2 Gen 2 at 10 Gbps means an SSD moves data at close to its native speed. The cable's USB 3.0 caps at 5 Gbps, half the speed for NVMe drives.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">The verdict</p>
    <p>Occasional use, SATA only, tight budget: cable. Frequent use, mixed SATA + NVMe, faster transfers matter: dock.</p><p>If you're a homelabber, buy the dock. If you're a normal person recovering files from one dead laptop, buy the cable and don't overthink it.</p>
  </section>

